"""        <h2>Useful Phrases <span class="em">🧰</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Ten phrases for building a friendship on purpose — new cities, new jobs, new stages of life. Say each out loud BEFORE you reveal it.</p>

        <div class="deliver"><b>Say it like you mean it.</b> The register here is warm and a little brave — every phrase risks a tiny bit of honesty, and the honesty is the mechanism. Keep the stakes small out loud ("an hour, done") precisely so the feelings underneath can be real. And expect the other person to be relieved: they almost always are.</div>

        <h3>🚪 Opening the door</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 1</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> naming what's been true for months. Complete it:<br><br>"We should do this ___ some time — not just in passing."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"We should do this properly some time — not just in passing."</b><br><b>In passing</b> = briefly, on the way to something else — which is exactly where good almost-friendships live and die. This sentence names the pattern ("this" — the chats you both already enjoy) and proposes upgrading it, without yet naming a date, which keeps it feather-light. It's the adult version of "want to play?" and it works for the same reason: it's simply true.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "We should do this properly some time." Car parks, kitchens, corridors — anywhere ten good minutes keep happening.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 2</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the brave admission that unlocks everything. Which version?</p>
            <div class="choice">
                <div><b>A.</b> "I'll be honest — I've been here a year and a half, and I don't know many people yet."</div>
                <div><b>B.</b> "I'm incredibly busy and popular, but I could probably fit you in."</div>
                <div><b>C.</b> "I have literally no friends. None. It's been quite dark, actually."</div>
            </div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the answer</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>A — "I'll be honest — I don't know many people yet."</b><br>B protects your image and kills the moment — unavailability is the exact opposite of an invitation. C hands a stranger more weight than the moment can carry. A is calibrated bravery: honest enough to give the other person permission to be honest back — and watch what Sam did with that permission: "Same, honestly." Almost everyone in a new city, new job or new life stage is quietly in the same boat; the admission is how two boats find each other.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'll be honest — I don't know many people here yet." The sentence loneliness least wants you to say, and most needs.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 3</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the invitation itself — small, specific, exit included. Complete it:<br><br>"Are you around Saturday? No big ___ — coffee by the market, an hour, done."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Are you around Saturday? No big plan — coffee by the market, an hour, done."</b><br>Anatomy of an acceptable invitation: a specific day (vague "some time" plans never happen), a small shape ("an hour, done" — they can see the whole commitment from here), and "no big plan" declaring the stakes low. Compare "we should organise a proper dinner some time" — enormous, vague, and therefore never. Small and specific beats grand and foggy every single time.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Are you around ___? No big plan — just ___." The invitation template for the rest of your life.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 4</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the pressure valve, the instant there's a complication. Complete it:<br><br>"No ___ at all — another time works too."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"No pressure at all — another time works too."</b><br>Say it fast and mean it — the moment their face clouds ("Saturday I've got the kids"), before any awkwardness can set. It does two things at once: releases them completely, and signals the invitation survives the complication. People can only relax into a friendship they're free to step out of; every gram of pressure now is a reason to avoid you later. And notice what freedom produced here: Sam suggested the workaround himself.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "No pressure at all — another time works too." Every invitation you'll ever make. Every single one.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🔧 Making the plan fit a real life</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 5</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> folding their complications in instead of waiting for a clear week. Complete it:<br><br>"Or ___ them, honestly, if that's easier."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Or bring them, honestly, if that's easier."</b><br>Adult lives are full — kids, shifts, elderly parents, the dog. Friendships that wait for everyone's calendar to clear are waiting forever. This move welcomes the complication instead: kids to the dinosaur thing, the dog on the walk, the partner to the pub quiz. It also says something bigger than logistics: I want to know your actual life, not the diary-gap version of you. That's what made Sam's face change.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Bring them / bring the dog / come after the shift — whatever's easier." Fit the friendship around the life, not the life around the friendship.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 6</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> capturing the contact before another two months of waving. Put the pieces in order:</p>
            <div class="jumble"><span>before one of us</span><span>Let me take your number</span><span>disappears.</span></div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Let me take your number before one of us disappears."</b><br>The unglamorous move the whole thing depends on: a plan without a contact channel is a rumour. "Before one of us disappears" makes it light — and it's true; gym schedules change, jobs move, and car-park friendships evaporate without a trace. Do it in the warm moment, on the spot, phone out. The number isn't forward; it's infrastructure.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Let me take your number before one of us disappears." The end of every good conversation with someone you'd like another one with.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🛟 Surviving the wobbles</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 7</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the check-in that isn't needy. Complete it:<br><br>"Still ___ for Saturday? No drama if the weekend's got complicated."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Still on for Saturday? No drama if the weekend's got complicated."</b><br>The Thursday message that keeps a Saturday plan alive — new plans with new people die of silence more than anything else, each side assuming the other's forgotten. "Still on?" is three words of glue; the second half keeps it weightless. This is maintenance, not neediness — and everyone is quietly grateful for the person who does it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Still on for ___?" Two days before, every loose plan, forever.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 8</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> answering the cancellation — graceful AND persistent. Complete it:<br><br>"No worries at all — but let's find another ___ rather than leave it floating. I ___ it."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"No worries at all — but let's find another day rather than leave it floating. I mean it."</b><br>The crossroads sentence. Plain "no worries!" is where new friendships go to die — both sides retreat, politely, forever. This version forgives the cancellation completely AND refuses to let the plan dissolve, and "I mean it" does real work: it tells them this wasn't politeness, the invitation was real. Then downsize if needed — Saturday coffee became Tuesday chips, and Tuesday chips actually happened. A smaller plan that happens beats a better plan that doesn't.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "No worries — but let's find another day. I mean it." The cancellation response that separates friendships from acquaintanceships.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 9</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> locking in the repeat while the first one's still warm. Complete it:<br><br>"This was fun — same ___ next ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"This was fun — same again next month?"</b><br>One coffee is an event; a repeat is a friendship. The easiest moment to book the second meeting is the last five minutes of the first — the evidence that it works is right there on the table between you. "Same again" also removes all planning cost: same place, same shape, known quantity. Rhythm is what friendship actually is, underneath — and rhythm starts with somebody proposing beat two.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Same again next month?" Before you part, every time it was good.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 10</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> what you say when it's you who has to decline — keeping the door wide open. Say it out loud, then write it.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="They've invited YOU, and you genuinely can't. Decline without closing anything…"></textarea>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal a strong version</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>e.g. "I can't do Saturday — but I'm not just saying that: what about the one after?"</b><br>The counter-offer is everything. A bare "can't make it, sorry!" is indistinguishable from a brush-off, however warmly you meant it — new connections can't yet tell your busy from your polite escape. Naming a specific alternative ("the one after") proves the interest survived the no. If you don't offer a new day, expect never to be asked again; if you do, you've just done your half of the building.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I can't do ___ — but what about ___?" Every decline of an invitation you're glad existed.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="keep">
            <h3>🔑 Make them yours</h3>
            <p>These ten build friendships on purpose, at any age, in any city. Say each answer out loud before you type it.</p>
            <p class="cue">"We should do this properly some time." — who is your Sam? The person you keep having ten good minutes with?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The person, the place, how long it's been going on…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"Are you around ___? No big plan — just ___." — build the actual invitation, for that actual person, this actual week.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Your invitation, small and specific…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"Let's find another day rather than leave it floating." — which plan in your life is currently floating? Rescue it, in one message, tonight.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The floating plan, and the message…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="modern_life_phrase_bank.html">🧰 <span>Every phrase from every Modern Life lesson, sorted by what it does.</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <div class="tip"><b>Quick test:</b> the whole arc, out loud — we should do this properly → I'll be honest, I don't know many people → are you around Saturday, no big plan → no pressure at all → bring them if it's easier → let me take your number → still on for Saturday? → no worries, but another day, I mean it → same again next month → can't do Saturday, what about the one after. Ten sentences between waving across a car park and a friend.</div>""",

