"""        <h2>The Echo <span class="em">🕰️</span></h2>

        <div class="echo-card">
            <p>Five thousand years ago, people walked for weeks to stand in a field in southern England. The archaeology at Stonehenge tells a story the postcards don't: the bones of pigs slaughtered in midwinter, isotopes showing animals driven from hundreds of miles away, the remains of enormous feasts at the nearby settlement. Whatever the stones meant, what people DID there was gather — eat together, at fixed times, year after year.</p>
            <p>Here's the part that matters for a Tuesday car park: the gathering wasn't spontaneous. It was engineered — a fixed place, a fixed rhythm, an unmissable reason to show up. Neolithic Britain solved adult friendship the way it has to be solved in every age: not by waiting for connection to happen, but by building the machinery that makes it inevitable. The stones are five thousand years of somebody going first, saying: here, at midwinter — everyone comes.</p>
            <p>Your version doesn't need forty tonnes of sarsen. It needs a coffee place by the market, a repeat that becomes a rhythm, and one person brave enough to propose the gathering. That's all Stonehenge ever was, underneath: the plan that refused to stay floating.</p>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="short_history_stonehenge.html">🗿 <span>Want the full story? The stones, the feasts, and the people who walked for weeks…</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <h3>Two bigger questions</h3>
        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Neolithic people built friendship into the calendar — festivals, feasts, fixed gatherings. What's modern life's equivalent, and why does it work worse?</li>
            <li>"Someone has to go first." In your life, who's gone first for you — and whose turn is it now?</li>
        </ol>""",
